      - 'node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply "{skill_target}" <the approved flags>  # the plan rerun: the same call without the write switch prints the plan and writes nothing, so the workflow reruns with repeated --approved-phrase, --description-trim, --allowed-tools, --edge and --agent-output and shows that plan'
      - 'node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs preserve --snapshot "{scratch_dir}/snapshot" "{scratch_dir}/staging"  # read-only: proves the agent-produced prose kept every original body line before the format gate, from the one scratch root'
      - 'node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply "{skill_target}" --apply  # the same plan, then the backup and the writes; the workflow adds the approved --approved-phrase, --description-trim, --allowed-tools, --edge and --agent-output flags and runs it only after an explicit yes'
      - 'node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-check.cjs .skilled/skills/<name> --json  # the post-write re-read through the read-only check engine, at the converted root: the staged root for a plain skill, the skill folder for an in-place conversion'
    trigger_phrases:
      - "convert a skill to the skilled format"
      - "convert a plain agent skill"
      - "turn my old skill into a skilled skill"
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DOCTOR: HELPER (READ-ONLY ROUTING)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
role: Diagnostic guide that turns a problem stated in plain words into the doctor command that fits, with the class and the reason on every line
purpose: Rank the doctor commands the route manifest describes against a described problem, then run a read-only pick in place or print a writing pick as one line for the operator to run themselves
action: Run .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-helper-match.cjs once -> render its candidates through the presentation contract -> run a read-only pick under its own router, or stop at a writing pick
skill_owner: ".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPERATING MODE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
operating_mode:
  workflow: single-step
  compliance: MANDATORY
  execution: read_only
  approvals: none
  tracking: single_run
  validation: read_only_assertion

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MUTATION BOUNDARIES (READ-ONLY)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
mutation_boundaries:
  read_only: true
  allowed_targets: []
  forbidden_targets:
    - "**/*"
  invariant: "This route ranks and stops. It reads the route manifest, runs the matcher once and prints. It MUST NOT run a target that writes, and it MUST NOT add an argument to a runnable it prints: every switch such as an apply or a fix flag is a decision that target's own gate owns. A pick whose class is read-only runs in place under that command's own router and frontmatter. A pick classed add-only or mutates is printed as one line for the operator to run, so that command's plan, its approval and its gate all run under its own frontmatter and never under this one."

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# UPSTREAM ASSETS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
upstream_assets:
  matcher: ".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-helper-match.cjs, run once in its text or json form"
  route_manifest: ".skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml, the one phrase table the matcher ranks from"
  presentation: ".skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-helper-presentation.txt"
  doctor_routers: ".skilled/commands/doctor/<name>.md, loaded by name only for a read-only pick"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# INPUTS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
inputs:
  target: "helper is the route's only target: /doctor:helper and /doctor:helper helper both run it, and no positional target exists."
  problem_text: "The problem in the operator's own words, taken from everything after -- so a pasted error line keeps its own dashes. Empty only when the three-question prompt is used instead."
  json_output: "true when --json is passed: the route prints the matcher's JSON and nothing else (default false)."
  area: "The doctor command named by the area answer, passed to the matcher as --area=<command> so only that command's routes rank (default none, so every route ranks)."
  intent: "check or change, taken from the third answer and passed as --intent= so a read-only route is preferred for check and a writing one for change (default none, so neither is preferred)."
  execution_mode: "INTERACTIVE"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXECUTION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
execution:
  steps:
    - "Run node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-helper-match.cjs once, passing the problem text after -- and adding --json, --area=<command> and --intent=check|change only when the operator supplied them."
    - "The matcher reads _routes.yaml, derives one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action exactly as the bridge deriver does, and scores each entry against the problem text. Its entries never include this route itself, and never include a private name."
    - "Each candidate carries its runnable line, its mutation class verbatim from the manifest, its score and the manifest phrase that best matched with the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence rather than invention."
    - "Render at most three candidates through the presentation contract. The class column decides what happens next, and the operator chooses by number."
    - "A pick whose class is read-only runs in place: load .skilled/commands/doctor/<name>.md with that route's target as its argument and follow that router from its first section, so its presentation contract and its workflow govern."
    - "A pick classed add-only or mutates is handed back: print the exact runnable line for the operator to run as its own command and stop. This route loads no writing workflow, adds no flag to the line, and never asks for an approval on the target's behalf."
    - "With no candidate above the floor, offer the read-only /doctor:setup fallback, which lists every doctor command and writes nothing."
    - "Map the matcher's exit code to the route status: 0 a match, 1 no candidate above the floor, 2 a bad argument or an unreadable manifest."

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OUTPUT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
output: |
  Exit 0 is a match: STATUS=OK MATCHES=[n], showing the DOCTOR HELPER header, the problem as quoted, the ranked list and the offer line.
  A writing pick closes on STATUS=OK ACTION=handoff: show the exact line the operator runs, and nothing else.
  Exit 1 is no candidate above the floor: STATUS=NO_MATCH FALLBACK=/doctor:setup, showing the no-match template and offering the read-only fallback.
  Exit 2 is a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing parser: STATUS=FAIL ERROR="[message]", naming the cause and the accepted invocation (/doctor:helper [<problem in plain words>] [--json]).
# /doctor:helper Presentation Contract

This file is the single source of truth for user-facing presentation in `/doctor:helper`: the argument failure, the three-question prompt, the ranked list, the offer to run, the no-match fallback, result templates and next-step text. The command router owns free-text binding and asset loading. The workflow YAML owns execution behavior.

## 1. Argument Failure

An unknown argument fails before any workflow YAML loads and prints the wording below with the bracketed parts filled in, then ends with the status line. Nothing is read or changed.

| Situation | Wording |
|-----------|---------|
| A flag other than a leading `--json` | `Unknown argument: [argument]` then `/doctor:helper takes --json and then your problem in plain words after --.` then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR="unknown_argument"` |
| An empty problem with no area answered | Re-emit the three-question prompt once. On a second unparseable reply, `STATUS=FAIL ERROR="unknown_selection"` |

## 2. No-Argument Prompt

With no argument, ask all three questions in one prompt, then wait. This follows the consolidated question protocol. The `1) Area:` line carries a placeholder and never the names: the list is replaced by the matcher's `--areas` output, whose entries are derived from the route manifest, so a new doctor command appears in the question with no edit to this file.

```text
Tell me what is wrong. Answer any or all in one reply, for example "2, commit blocked, change".

1) Area:  [the area list, printed from the manifest: one N) name per doctor command] or "not sure"
2) What happened? An error line or a few words.
3) Check only, or change something?  check / change / not sure
```

Accepted answers:

| Answer | Result | Meaning |
|--------|--------|---------|
| A number or a command name | `--area=<command>` | Rank only that doctor command's routes, in its own tie-break order when no problem text follows |
| `not sure`, empty, `any` | no area filter | Rank every doctor command |
| Any text | the problem text, passed after `--` | Rank against that description |
| `check` | `--intent=check` | Prefer a route that only reads |
| `change` | `--intent=change` | Prefer a route that writes |

The area list is printed from the manifest at run time: this contract holds a placeholder for it and never the command names, so a new doctor command needs no edit here. On any other response, re-emit the prompt once. On a second invalid response, return `STATUS=FAIL ERROR="unknown_selection"`.

## 3. Ranked List

Render the matcher's text output through this template. The class column is the manifest's value for that route, verbatim, and the reason quotes the manifest phrase the matcher named, so the line is repeatable evidence. The class always starts at least two spaces after the longest runnable printed, so a long `/doctor:` runnable never runs into it and the operator can copy the line as it stands.

```text
DOCTOR HELPER
Problem: "[the problem as the operator wrote it]"

1) [runnable]       [read-only|add-only|mutates]   matches "[manifest phrase]" ([matched terms])
2) [runnable]       [read-only|add-only|mutates]   matches "[manifest phrase]" ([matched terms])
3) [runnable]       [read-only|add-only|mutates]   matches "[manifest phrase]" ([matched terms])

Run one? Reply 1-3, or X to stop.
STATUS=OK MATCHES=[n]
```

Show at most three lines. Drop the header when `--json` was passed, because the matcher's JSON is the whole answer then.

A standalone action entry is ranked like a route: `/doctor:update compat` is its own line, and the bare `/doctor:update` shows the class of the check action the bare command runs.

## 4. The Offer to Run

The class on the chosen line decides the next step, and the operator never gets a surprise.

- **A read-only pick runs in place.** Load `.skilled/commands/doctor/<name>.md` with that route's target as its argument and follow it from its first section, so that command's own presentation contract and workflow govern the run. This command adds nothing to it.
- **A pick classed add-only or mutates is shown for you to run.** Print the exact runnable line, for example `/doctor:git hooks`, and stop with `STATUS=OK ACTION=handoff`. A writing target's plan, its own approval and its own gate then run under its own frontmatter, which is the only place they belong.

This command never appends an argument to a runnable line. A target that needs a switch asks for it itself, under its own gate.

## 5. No Match

When the matcher exits 1, nothing scored above the floor. Say so plainly and offer the one read-only command that lists the whole family.

```text
No doctor command matched "[the problem]".
/doctor:setup lists every doctor command and runs the read-only checks. Run it now? Y / X
STATUS=NO_MATCH FALLBACK=/doctor:setup
```

`/doctor:setup` only reads, so a `Y` runs it in place under the rule in the previous section. An `X` stops with `STATUS=CANCELLED ACTION=cancelled`.

## 6. Result Templates

### Summary

```text
DOCTOR HELPER RESULT
Problem: "[the problem]"
Status: [OK|NO_MATCH|ERROR]
Candidates: [n] shown of [n] above the floor
Choice: [the runnable line, or none]
Action: [ran in place|handed back|offered the fallback|stopped]
Mutation class: [the chosen route's class, or none]
Evidence: [the manifest phrase and the matched terms, quoted]
STATUS=OK MATCHES=[n]
```

### Failure Summary

```text
DOCTOR TARGET FAILED
Target: helper
Step: [the step that failed]
Error: [message]
Next: [the accepted invocation, or the manifest command to rerun]
STATUS=FAIL ERROR="[message]"
```

## 7. Troubleshooting Display

| Situation | Suggested Action |
|-----------|------------------|
| The ranked list names a route you did not expect | Read the quoted phrase and the matched terms: the line says which manifest wording it ranked on. Rerun `/doctor:helper -- "the same problem in more words"` |
| No candidate above the floor | Answer the three questions, or describe the symptom with the words the tool prints. The fallback lists every doctor command |
| The matcher exits 2 | The problem text or the manifest failed. Run `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh` for the manifest |
| A writing pick was handed back | Run the printed line on its own. That command plans, asks for its own approval and writes under its own gate |
| The helper names the family you meant but not the route | The route's `trigger_phrases` in `_routes.yaml` are missing your wording. Add one there and rerun the reach probe |

End interactive presentations with: `What would you like to do next?`

