.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/scripts/README.md:13:`scripts/` holds the one drift-guard entrypoint for the `sk-code-skilled` surface. It runs four drift guards (alignment-drift, stack-folder, router-sync, doc-claims) in sequence and exits non-zero if any fails, so a completion gate never has to remember separate commands. The router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`) restores the four checks of the router-sync suite that was deleted with its lane: (1) every path in the machine-readable router exists on disk, every routable reference or asset doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed; (2) the parent surface RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier; (3) compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and the sk-code-skilled RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`; (4) every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router. The entrypoint runs every leg: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc part of (1), skips paths Git ignores and reports any remaining reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink. If Git is missing or the hub is not a repository, leg 1b keeps the full walked list. The dead-path part of (1) is `verify_alignment_drift.py --check-router`, which this entrypoint also runs. `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4), in CI only, in warn-only mode. The doc-claims guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) checks the sk-code prose: path references resolve, no retired packet name remains, every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json` and the `ROUTER.md` load-tier claims match `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.
